"Bounds for higher Steklov and mixed Steklov Neumann eigenvalues on domains with holes · Pith (https://www.pith.science/paper/2412.17124)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.9 years ago; Crawled: last month; arxiv 2412.17124 v1 pith:VDS3P7AC submitted 2024-12-22 math.SP ...   * Without order-4 symmetry the theorem is false: the paper's computed examples of a rectangle with a circular hole and an ellipse with a circular hole (both only order-2 symmetric) have second Steklov and second mixed Steklov–Neumann eigenvalues larger than those of the corresponding annulus, so symmetry is not a technical convenience but a necessary hypothesis. ... Each label is drawn from a closed vocabulary; the number beside it is the probability the classifier gave that label. ... Figures reproduced from arXiv: 2412.17124 by the authors.\n### A structured set of objections, weighed in public.\n\nDesk editor's note, referee report, and a circularity audit.\n\nDesk Editor's Note read the letter\n\nTwo things to know before you read it. The paper has two real new contributions: an explicit formula for the second distinct Steklov eigenvalue on a concentric annulus (Theorem 2.1), and sharp volume-normalized upper bounds for the first n Steklov and mixed Steklov–Neumann eigenvalues on order-4 symmetric domains with a central hole (Theorems 4.1 and 5.1). The test-space construction is the standard Szegő–Weinberger one, applied cleanly, and the numerical section is presented honestly as observations and conjectures.\n\nWhat I want you to know is that the proofs as written do not support the claims. Theorem 2.1 relies on Lemma 2.3, which contains a visibly false identity: the text uses (2 + L^n) = 2 + L(n−2) + 2L (= 2 + nL). That equality is wrong for general L>1, n≥3, and the resulting inequality 2+L^n ≥ n+2L is also false for L close to 1. Lemma 2.4 is then dismissed with 'similar technique' and no proof. So the main explicit-eigenvalue result is not established.\n\nThe two isoperimetric theorems are structurally plausible but depend on Lemma 3.2 and several symmetry-lemmas cited to the authors' unreviewed preprint [9]. No proofs are reproduced. That alone makes the central results conditional. There is also a smaller gap in Lemma 3.3: the proof needs the inequality ∫_{shell} G dV ≥ ∫_Ω G dV, which requires G ≥ 0. The paper only proves G is increasing. The stress-test note claims the shell integral presumes star-shapedness—I think that is a misreading: the displayed equality is actually an inequality, and Ω is always contained in the radial shell because R_u is the sup radius in direction u. But the positivity of G is genuinely missing.\n\nBottom line: the ideas are worth a serious referee, and the flaws are localized and repairable. I would send it out for peer review, not desk-reject it, but the revision needs to include proofs of the lemmas from [9], fix Lemma 2.3/2.4, and handle G's sign. As it stands, I would not cite it.\n\n#### Referee Report\n\n4 major / 4 minor\n\nSummary. The paper studies Steklov and mixed Steklov–Neumann eigenvalues on smooth bounded domains in R^n (n≥2) that contain a central spherical hole. It claims (i) an explicit formula for the second nonzero Steklov eigenvalue, counted without multiplicity, on a concentric annulus (Theorem 2.1); (ii) a sharp upper bound σ_k(Ω) ≤ σ_k(Ω0) = σ_1(Ω0) for k=1,...,n when Ω = Ω_out \\setminus \\bar{B}_1 is symmetric of order 4 and Ω0 is the concentric annular domain of equal volume (Theorem 4.1); and (iii) an analogous bound for mixed Steklov–Neumann eigenvalues (Theorem 5.1). The paper also gives numerical examples intended to show that the order-4 symmetry assumption cannot be dropped, and numerical data leading to two conjectures.\n\nThe theorem assumes only that Ω_out is smooth, bounded, connected, and symmetric of order 4, with \\bar{B}_1 ⊂ Ω_out; it does not assume Ω is star-shaped or radially monotone. For a non-star-shaped order-4 domain, such as a ball with order-4-symmetric reentrant bays or holes, a radial ray can pass through points outside Ω or Ω can contain points not captured by the outermost radius R_u, so the displayed equality is false. Consequently Corollary 3.1, which is used directly at the end of the proof of Theorem 4.1, is not justified by the submitted text.\n  2. [§2.3, Lemma 2.3] The proof of Lemma 2.3 uses the identity (2 + L^n) = 2 + L(n-2) + 2L for n ≥ 3. This is algebraically false: for L > 1 and n ≥ 3, L^n is not equal to L(n-2) + 2L. The subsequent inequality (2.9) therefore is not derived by the displayed argument. The claim σ_{2,1} ≤ σ_{0,2} may be true, but it is not established by this proof, leaving Theorem 2.1 unsupported for n ≥ 3.\n  3. [§2.3, Lemma 2.4] Lemma 2.4 is asserted with 'Applying the similar technique as in the proof of Lemma 2.2, it can be proved that h̃(L) ≥ 0' and no rigorous proof is given. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConjectured eigenvalue monotonicity for an ellipse with an off-center hole | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/366359/conjectured-eigenvalue-monotonicity-for-an-ellipse-with-an-o)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Ellipse-hole eigenvalue monotonicity conjecture. ... Sagar Basak and Sheela Verma, “Bounds for higher Steklov and mixed Steklov Neumann eigenvalues on domains with holes”, arXiv:2412.17124 (2024).\n\n# Conjectured eigenvalue monotonicity for an ellipse with an off-center hole\n\nAbout 2 years old · traced to 2024\n\nLet $\\Omega_{out}$Ω o u t​ be the ellipse $E_{r,R}$E r,R​ in $\\mathbb{R}^{2}$R 2 centered at the origin, with minor axis $r$r and major axis $R$R, and let $\\Omega_{(t_1,t_2)}=\\Omega_{out}\\setminus B_1(t_1,t_2)$Ω(t 1​,t 2​)​=Ω o u t​∖B 1​(t 1​,t 2​), where $B_1(t_1,t_2)$B 1​(t 1​,t 2​) is a unit ball contained in the ellipse. The distance between the centers is the distance from the origin to $(t_1,t_2)$(t 1​,t 2​).\n\nEllipse-hole eigenvalue monotonicity conjecture. The eigenvalues $\\sigma_1(\\Omega_{(t_1,t_2)})$σ 1​(Ω(t 1​,t 2​)​) and $\\mu_2(\\Omega_{(t_1,t_2)})$μ 2​(Ω(t 1​,t 2​)​) decrease with respect to the distance between the centers of $E_{r,R}$E r,R​ and $B_1(t_1,t_2)$B 1​(t 1​,t 2​). The eigenvalue $\\mu_1(\\Omega_{(t_1,t_2)})$μ 1​(Ω(t 1​,t 2​)​) decreases with respect to that distance when the center of $B_1(t_1,t_2)$B 1​(t 1​,t 2​) varies along the lines $y=0$y=0 and $y=x$y=x, but increases when the center varies along the line $x=0$x=0.\n\nThese claims are based on numerical observations for an ellipse as outer domain and are stated for the specified directions of motion. Their general validity is not established in the supplied text.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nSagar Basak and Sheela Verma, “Bounds for higher Steklov and mixed Steklov Neumann eigenvalues on domains with holes”, arXiv:2412.17124 (2024).\n\neigenvalue-monotonicityspectral-geometrysteklov-eigenvalues\n\n0 0\n\nPosted 1 month ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBounds for higher Steklov and mixed Steklov Neumann eigenvalues on domains with holes | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/387351144_Bounds_for_higher_Steklov_and_mixed_Steklov_Neumann_eigenvalues_on_domains_with_holes)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; DOI:10.48550/arXiv.2412.17124 ... Next, we consider the mixed Steklov Neumann eigenvalue problem on $4^{\\text{th}}$ order symmetric domains in $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ having a spherical hole and obtain upper bound of the first n nonzero eigenvalues.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Jean_Philippe_M in:math - SciRate Search (h